- op: discuss  # 39f3
  at: '2026-08-29T07:17:15Z'  # 39f3
  by: agent  # 39f3
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 39f3
  clock: 4  # 39f3
  nonce: 39f3  # 39f3
  message: "RULED — ADR-0049, 'A Deferred Crossing Is Disclosed, Not Minted' (extends ADR-0016). Accepted 2026-08-29.\n\nWHAT THIS ITEM ASKED FOR AND WHAT IT GOT. The ask was one adjudication for the whole family instead of nine implicit ones, with two branches offered: (a) a launch call IS a receive, or (b) it is not and all rows move together. The ruling is neither branch as framed, and the reason is that both assumed the choice was between minting a source and having nothing. ADR-0016 already ruled the OUTBOUND twin of this question five months ago -- INV-vavup forbids attributing a launched program's I/O to the launcher, and installs command_launch to DISCLOSE a crossing rather than resolve it. Server launch is that same mis-attribution pointed inward. There is a third state, it is already ratified in this tree, and this item's framing did not have it.\n\nTHE RULING. A boundary tag asserts the crossing happens AT THAT CALL, IN THAT CALL'S SCOPE. The discriminator: does the call return -- or write into a caller-visible location -- a value whose content is chosen by the party on the far side? Yes => a TRANSFER. No, and the call merely opens/registers/subscribes/schedules/defers => a DEFERRED CROSSING, which is disclosed and never minted. Not new law: it is exactly what removed Haskell's gracefulClose ('returns unit, so no byte reaches the program') and what WI-dosov applied when it dropped socket/bind/listen while keeping accept. ADR-0049 states the rule those were instances of.\n\nTHREE THINGS THIS ITEM GOT WRONG, ALL CORRECTED IN THE ADR.\n\n1. THE CENSUS. This item claims 37 rows / 9 languages. Its own enumeration sums to 52. Two independent re-counts under different membership rules produced 63 and 75/13. Four numbers, four answers -- because there was no written membership rule to count against, so every sweep invented one. Confirmed missing from the item's list: erlang (gen_tcp.accept, ssl.transport_accept, httpd.start), rust (TcpListener.accept), c/cpp (sys/socket.accept), go (echo.Echo.Start/StartTLS). That is the hardcoded-9-languages defect this item cites INV-kanuk for, recurring inside the item itself. Filed as WI-hazop.\n\n2. 'DO NOT ADJUDICATE A SUBSET' WAS RIGHT ABOUT CONVENIENCE-SPLITTING AND WRONG ABOUT THIS FAMILY. It is four mechanisms, not one: setup (socket/bind/listen), blocking serve loop (ListenAndServe/serve_forever/Warp.run), per-connection accept, and route-registration DSL (Phoenix.Router). Under the discriminator they separate cleanly, and ACCEPT IS NOT A MEMBER -- it returns a peer-chosen address to its caller, so it is a transfer and stays net_recv on its own merits. Ruling the four together would have taken accept with them, which is the env_read error INV-tutar closed. accept is now asserted separately across seven languages, explicitly because the launch rows and accept survive for OPPOSITE reasons.\n\n3. THE HEADLINE PYTHON EXAMPLE IS INERT. flask.Flask.run is in python's ambiguous_names; a bare app.run() yields ZERO edges and total_io_edges 0. It has never minted a taint source. serve_forever, uvicorn.run and asyncio.start_server DO fire, so this is per-row, not a language-wide gap. A row can be argued about for an entire panel while minting nothing -- which is why reachability now gates adjudication (WI-vapud) rather than following it.\n\nNO ROW MOVED AND NO OUTPUT CHANGED. ADR-0049 ruling 3 licenses a retag only against a represented crossing (the WI-lunav rule: dropping a row that transmitted nothing, whose every counterpart was catalogued, still collapsed a real violated(3) to inconclusive(0)). TestServerLaunchStaysAReceive remains the pin against a silent split; its docstring is demoted from BEING the ruling to CITING it, which was this item's actual complaint -- the only governing text for a nine-language family was a test docstring.\n\nTHE HALF THAT IS EASY TO OMIT, RECORDED SO IT CANNOT BE. A disclosure boundary MUST be opacity-gating. Since INV-buzab a classified call is what 'examined' means, so a disclosure tag that mints nothing still counts as examined and returns a green tick over live ingress. ADR-0016's own table measures this in the outbound direction: cataloguing curl as net_send moved a cron-dropper claim from inconclusive rc 2 to confirmed rc 0. OPAQUE_BOUNDARIES anticipates the membership in its governing sentence but its EXAMPLE says 'control left this process' -- the outbound instance -- so the extension to the inbound case is made explicitly in the ADR rather than by silent reading.\n\nROOT CAUSE, FILED SEPARATELY AS INV-tafig. io_boundary is absent from _known_axes(): no registry, no axiom, no drift linter, while ADR-0016 and taint.py both call it 'hypergumbo's canonical I/O-boundary risk taxonomy' and four consumers branch on its values. That absence is WHY this question was derived four times and ruled zero times -- there was nowhere a ruling could live. It is the condition under which env_read split (INV-tutar, 134 of 195 rows misclassified, 22.9% precision).\n\nFOLLOW-ONS: WI-hazop (census, blocks the rest) -> WI-vapud (reachability per row) -> WI-nosah (opacity-gated disclosure boundary) + WI-ritog (arrival-scope seeding; start_at:callee, dispatches_to and entrypoints all exist and taint.py references none of them). INV-tafig (declare the axis) is independent and should land before the row moves. INV-kanuk is unblocked by this item reaching a resolved state; note that measurement 0006 already carries an adjudicated verdict against c's socket row, so part of INV-kanuk may fall out of the census directly.\n\nREVERSIBLE. An ADR can be superseded and no catalogue row has moved. If the reachability pass shows the family is largely inert, this becomes a correctness cleanup rather than a precision fix -- which changes its priority, not its verdict."  # 39f3
